ress><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>QU WEIFENG</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>SAITO SHIGERU</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>ABE, TAKAO</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>AIHARA, KEN</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>AKIYAMA, SHOJI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>HAYAMIZU, YOSHINORI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>IGARASHI, TETSUYA</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>QU, WEIFENG</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>SAITO, SHIGERU</name></addressbook></inventor></inventors></parties></bibliographic-data><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="EN"><p>A silicon wafer production method comprising the steps of growing, by a Czochralski method, a silicon single crystal bar having a resistivity of at least 100  OMEGA .cm and an initial interstitial oxygen concentration of 10 to 25 ppma, processing the silicon single crystal bar into a wafer, and oxygen-precipitation-heat-treating the wafer to reduce a residual interstitial oxygen concentration in the wafer to up to 8 ppma. A silicon wafer produced as above is small in resistivity reduction when heat-treated during a device production or the like. A silicon wafer produced and heat-treated to have the above initial interstitial oxygen concentration and residual interstitial oxygen concentration independently of resistivity can eliminate slip dislocation in the subsequent heat treating. An epitaxial layer formed on the surface of the silicon wafer can form a high-resistivity epitaxial wafer free from slip dislocation and applicable to various devices.</p></abstract><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="FR"><p>L'invention concerne un procédé de production d'une tranche de silicium avec accroissement par la méthode de Czochralski d'un barreau monocristallin dont la résistivité est d'au moins 100  OMEGA .cm et dont la teneur en oxygène interstitielle initiale de 10 à 25 ppma, à traiter le barreau de silicium monocristallin pour en faire une tranche et à soumettre la tranche à un traitement thermique de précipitation d'oxygène afin de réduire la teneur en oxygène interstitielle résiduelle dans la tranche à 8 ppma. La résistivité d'une telle tranche de silicium n'est pratiquement pas affectée lors du traitement thermique pendant la production d'un dispositif ou analogue. L'invention concerne une tranche de silicium dont la teneur en oxygène interstitielle initiale et la teneur en oxygène interstitielle résiduelle, indépendamment de sa résistivité, peut éliminer la dislocation lors du traitement thermique subséquent. Une couche épitaxiale formée sur la surface de la tranche de silicium peut former une tranche épitaxiale à forte résistivité sans risque de dislocation et applicable dans différents dispositifs.</p></abstract><copyright>User acknowledges that the Information Retrieval Facility (IRF) and its third party providers retain all right, title and interest in and to this xml under applicable copyright laws. User acquires no ownership rights to this xml including but not limited to its format. User hereby accepts the terms and conditions of the Licence Agreement set forth at http://www.ir-facility.org/legal/marec/data_licence</copyright></patent-document>